**=================================================================================================
** Bonded anchor pry-out test -- EXPLICIT DYNAMIC (NED) variant of test.inp
**=================================================================================================
**
** Derived from test.inp (implicit, NISTParallel + blockamg) by the changes recorded in
** PLAN_EXPLICIT_PRYOUT.md section 4. Read that document before changing anything here; several of
** the values below are not free parameters but consequences of the explicit time increment.
**
** CONSISTENT UNITS: N, mm, MPa, tonne, s. Every density below follows from that choice.
**
** WHAT CHANGED, and why:
**
**  1. Densities. Required now: the solver builds a lumped mass from getDensity() on every element.
**     Concrete 3e-9, steel 7.85e-9, mortar/grout 2.2e-9 t/mm^3. The concrete value was already in
**     the file but was NOT being read -- GCDP's getDensity() indexed the same property slot
**     computeStress() reads the Duvaut-Lions viscosity from, so it returned 1e-6 and the concrete
**     came out 333x too heavy. Fixed in the GCDP module (fix/density-property-index); the property
**     list below is now 21 entries, density at idx 19 and nonlocal viscosity at idx 20.
**
**  2. The Duvaut-Lions viscosity is ZERO here, down from 1e-6. It is a relaxation time. At an
**     implicit increment of ~5e-3 s, dT/eta ~ 5000 and it acts as mild numerical regularisation; at
**     the explicit increment of ~2e-7 s, dT/eta = 0.2 means almost nothing relaxes per increment and
**     the concrete responds very nearly ELASTICALLY -- a run that completes, looks plausible, and
**     shows no softening. Explicit integration needs no Newton to stabilise, so zero is right.
**
**  3. The nonlocal viscosity (new, idx 20) is the mass of the nonlocal damage field's forward-Euler
**     update -- it is what makes an elliptic regularisation field explicitly integrable. It also
**     sets that field's own stability limit, roughly dT <~ 2*eta_nl/(1 + l^2/h^2), and NOTHING
**     CHECKS IT: the element's computeCriticalTimeStepForExplicitDynamics carries an explicit
**     "TODO: current implementation ignores nonlocal variables". With l = 2 mm and the post-
**     refinement h = 1.14 mm, l^2/h^2 = 3.1 and dT ~ 2e-7, so the floor is ~4e-7. 1e-5 is ~24x above
**     it. Size this against the FINEST element the run can reach, not the initial mesh.
**
**  4. Solver NEDParallel, with the displacement field on central differences and the nonlocal damage
**     field on forward Euler. No linear solver: an explicit increment solves no system, so
**     blockamg.json is irrelevant here.
**
**  5. stepLength is now PHYSICAL SECONDS, not the implicit model's dimensionless 1. The critical
**     time step is set by the 1.4 mm steel elements, not the concrete: the mesh gives
**     dt_crit = 2.45e-7 s, and refining the concrete borehole (splitFactor=3) takes it to 3.41e-7 s,
**     still above the steel limit -- so AMR here costs NO extra increments. maxNumInc, not
**     stepLength, is what bounds a smoke test.
**
**  6. A quintic smoothstep amplitude on the loading Dirichlet, so the plate starts from rest with
**     zero velocity AND zero acceleration. The implicit file's header claimed one but the block had
**     none; under implicit statics that was harmless, here it would shock the structure.
**
**  7. The warm-up step is gone -- it exists to settle tie/contact state for a Newton solve, and a
**     single explicit increment of 2e-7 s settles nothing.
**
**  8. The live stress marker is gone from the AMR block; only the initialOnly geometric one remains.
**     This solver runs the topology update exactly once, before its increment loop, and refuses
**     modifiers that would act later BY NAME rather than silently not running them. Note also that
**     an instantaneous-stress threshold is the wrong criterion for an explicit run in any case: the
**     stress carries the full wave content and there is no coarsening anywhere in the code, so it
**     ratchets toward refining the whole element set. See PLAN_LIVE_AMR_EXPLICIT.md section 5.
**
** UNCHANGED from test.inp: the mesh, all node/element sets, all 5 ties, both contact pairs, and
** every Dirichlet boundary condition -- including the symmetry-plane condition, which is applied
** on the complete z_symm_concrete (4335 nodes) / z_symm_steel (924 nodes) sets carried by the mesh
** files themselves, exactly as test.inp does. Applying it on an INCOMPLETE set leaves symmetry-plane
** nodes free to move out of plane, which does not fail -- it silently changes the answer, and cost a
** full production run to find. The solver reconciles the resulting Dirichlet/tie overlap itself, so
** no hand-diffed subtraction of tie-slave nodes is needed. The simplification notes 1-7 in
** test.inp's own header still apply and are not repeated here.
**
**=================================================================================================
** MESH
**=================================================================================================
*include, input=./mesh/concrete_edelweissfe.inp
*include, input=./mesh/steel_edelweissfe.inp

